# UI

The arithmetic and the wording a list view needs.

★ WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE. A toast and a modal are a VIEW plus state plus a
dismissal action — in this language that is a `## Component`, not a `## To`. Shipping
them as functions would mean returning markup as `Text`, which is the one shape the
framework spends its whole design avoiding: it is how a page starts concatenating HTML
and how `{raw}` becomes routine. What belongs here is the part that genuinely is a
function.

★ AND THAT PART IS WHERE THE BUGS ARE. Pagination is off-by-one country: LOGOS sequences
are 1-INDEXED, a page count is a ceiling division, an empty collection still has a page
to show, and the last page is short. Each is one line, and each is wrong in somebody's
shipped application right now.

`examples/data_table` pages a filtered, sorted table with these functions: `clampPage`
keeps the requested page inside the table, and `pageStart` to `pageEnd` is the 1-based
run of rows that page shows, copied out with `item i of rows`. The UI section of
`docs/lgx-stdlib.md` builds a pager the same way and uses the text helpers as filters.

## Note
How many pages `total` items make at `perPage` each.

Never 0: an empty list still renders — it shows "nothing here" — and a count of 0 makes
every page number clamp to 0, which indexes nothing and reads as a bug to whoever is
looking at an empty screen wondering why.

## To pageCount (total: Int) and (perPage: Int) -> Int:
    If perPage is at most 0:
        Return 1.
    If total is at most 0:
        Return 1.
    Let whole be total / perPage.
    If whole * perPage is equal to total:
        Return whole.
    Return whole + 1.

## Note
`page` brought inside `1` to `pageCount(total, perPage)`.

## To clampPage (page: Int) and (total: Int) and (perPage: Int) -> Int:
    Let last be pageCount(total, perPage).
    If page is at most 1:
        Return 1.
    If page is greater than last:
        Return last.
    Return page.

## Note
The 1-based index of the first item on `page`.

1-based because LOGOS sequences are. A module answering 0-based bounds would be correct
in isolation and wrong at every call site, which is the worst kind of correct.

## To pageStart (page: Int) and (perPage: Int) -> Int:
    If page is at most 1:
        Return 1.
    Return (page - 1) * perPage + 1.

## Note
The 1-based index of the last item on `page`, never past `total`.

For an empty collection this is 0 while `pageStart` is 1 — an EMPTY range, deliberately,
so a caller's loop simply does not run rather than reading item 1 of nothing and trapping.

## To pageEnd (page: Int) and (perPage: Int) and (total: Int) -> Int:
    Let stop be pageStart(page, perPage) + perPage - 1.
    If stop is greater than total:
        Return total.
    Return stop.

## Note
`t` cut to at most `n` characters, marked with `…` when anything was removed.

A cut with no mark is a lie about the content: the reader cannot tell `Ada` from `Ada…`.
The mark costs one of the `n`, so the answer is never longer than asked.

## To truncate (t: Text) and (n: Int) -> Text:
    If n is at most 0:
        Return "".
    If length of t is at most n:
        Return t.
    Let mutable out be "".
    Let mutable i be 0.
    For each c in t:
        Set i to i + 1.
        If i is less than n:
            Set out to out + c.
    Return out + "…".

## Note
`n` with the word that agrees with it: `1 item`, `2 items`, `0 items`.

## To plural (n: Int) and (one: Text) and (many: Text) -> Text:
    If n is equal to 1:
        Return "{n} {one}".
    Return "{n} {many}".

## Note
The first letter of each word of `name`, run together: `Ada Lovelace` is `AL`.

Runs of whitespace are one separator and leading or trailing space contributes nothing,
so a name a visitor typed with a stray space still reads correctly.

## To initials (name: Text) -> Text:
    Let mutable out be "".
    Let mutable fresh be true.
    For each c in name:
        If c is equal to " ":
            Set fresh to true.
        Otherwise:
            If fresh is equal to true:
                Set out to out + c.
                Set fresh to false.
    Return out.
